1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,575
I built a RAG system that runs entirely on
my laptop.

2
00:00:04,925 --> 00:00:08,625
It ended up with a retrieval score of
ninety-five percent.

3
00:00:08,975 --> 00:00:11,750
But the interesting part isn't what worked.

4
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:16,913
Three of the techniques everyone recommends,
HyDE, fine-tuning, and a better

5
00:00:16,913 --> 00:00:18,750
embedding model, did nothing.

6
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:24,300
And one tiny bug in a tool definition cut my
results by sixty percent.

7
00:00:24,650 --> 00:00:27,300
Here's the whole story, with the numbers.

8
00:00:27,930 --> 00:00:30,605
The project is a help-desk assistant.

9
00:00:30,955 --> 00:00:35,933
You describe an IT problem, and it searches
past tickets and tells you what

10
00:00:35,933 --> 00:00:36,530
they say.

11
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:40,280
Everything is local on an M2 MacBook Pro.

12
00:00:40,630 --> 00:00:45,922
Ollama runs the models: qwen3 eight-billion
for generation and nomic for

13
00:00:45,922 --> 00:00:46,730
embeddings.

14
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:49,730
Qdrant is the vector database.

15
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:52,705
A cross-encoder reranks results.

16
00:00:53,055 --> 00:00:55,330
Phoenix traces every step.

17
00:00:55,680 --> 00:01:01,080
And at the end, ollmcp lets a local model
decide when to search.

18
00:01:01,430 --> 00:01:06,350
The data is a synthetic ticket dataset from
Hugging Face, a thousand tickets

19
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:07,580
in the free sample.

20
00:01:07,930 --> 00:01:10,355
Here is the path a question takes.

21
00:01:10,705 --> 00:01:15,057
It gets rewritten, then searched two ways at
once, then grouped into

22
00:01:15,057 --> 00:01:19,730
distinct problems, reranked, filtered, and
only then handed to the model.

23
00:01:20,363 --> 00:01:22,113
First surprise.

24
00:01:22,463 --> 00:01:26,513
A thousand tickets, but only fifty-six
distinct problems.

25
00:01:26,863 --> 00:01:31,638
Each one is repeated ten to thirty times
with different names attached.

26
00:01:31,988 --> 00:01:36,497
The comments are even worse: twenty-four
distinct sentences, attached to

27
00:01:36,497 --> 00:01:38,438
tickets more or less at random.

28
00:01:38,788 --> 00:01:43,913
And there is no real resolution field, so I
can't trust any of it as a fix.

29
00:01:44,263 --> 00:01:46,838
It's a ticket search and triage tool.

30
00:01:47,188 --> 00:01:50,788
That one fact shaped almost every decision
after this.

31
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:55,915
I searched for "printer" with three methods:
embeddings, keyword search, and

32
00:01:55,915 --> 00:01:56,920
the two combined.

33
00:01:57,270 --> 00:02:02,473
Every single result, in all three, was the
same problem: label printer feeds

34
00:02:02,473 --> 00:02:03,295
blank stock.

35
00:02:03,645 --> 00:02:06,820
But the data has three different printer
problems.

36
00:02:07,170 --> 00:02:11,497
The biggest cluster of near-duplicates
filled the whole top of the list, and

37
00:02:11,497 --> 00:02:13,945
I'd never have known the other two existed.

38
00:02:14,295 --> 00:02:16,495
Averages would have hidden this.

39
00:02:16,845 --> 00:02:19,970
I only saw it by reading the actual results.

40
00:02:20,578 --> 00:02:25,192
The fix was to stop retrieving tickets and
start retrieving distinct

41
00:02:25,192 --> 00:02:25,803
problems.

42
00:02:26,153 --> 00:02:30,490
Qdrant can group by a field, so I asked for
the top twenty different

43
00:02:30,490 --> 00:02:31,128
summaries.

44
00:02:31,478 --> 00:02:36,109
The chance that the right problem was even
in the candidate pool jumped from

45
00:02:36,109 --> 00:02:38,303
seventy-four to ninety-five percent.

46
00:02:38,653 --> 00:02:42,865
Then a cross-encoder reranker, reading the
question and each summary

47
00:02:42,865 --> 00:02:47,139
together, took the right answer in the top
five from about seventy to

48
00:02:47,139 --> 00:02:48,378
ninety-five percent.

49
00:02:48,728 --> 00:02:53,658
One lesson inside that: reranking on the
short summary beat reranking on the

50
00:02:53,658 --> 00:02:58,328
full ticket text, because the full text had
random noise attached to it.

51
00:02:58,678 --> 00:03:03,438
The real lesson: retrieval can't recover
what it never fetched, so measure

52
00:03:03,438 --> 00:03:04,853
each stage separately.

53
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:07,705
Now the things that didn't work.

54
00:03:08,055 --> 00:03:12,517
HyDE, where the model writes a fake answer
to search with: no gain, and it

55
00:03:12,517 --> 00:03:15,230
got worse when I let it replace the
question.

56
00:03:15,580 --> 00:03:20,104
Fine-tuning the embedding model: it trained
fine, and changed nothing,

57
00:03:20,104 --> 00:03:24,305
because the untouched model was already
finding the right answer.

58
00:03:24,655 --> 00:03:29,496
Switching to a better embedder: dramatically
better on its own, and exactly

59
00:03:29,496 --> 00:03:32,530
the same once the reranker was in the
pipeline.

60
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:35,205
The reranker erased the difference.

61
00:03:35,555 --> 00:03:40,334
And a score cutoff I'd tuned by eye was
silently throwing away correct

62
00:03:40,334 --> 00:03:40,880
results.

63
00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:43,180
Components interact.

64
00:03:43,530 --> 00:03:47,580
A win in isolation doesn't predict a win in
the system.

65
00:03:48,206 --> 00:03:50,481
How do you know any of this is real?

66
00:03:51,031 --> 00:03:55,805
My first set of test questions was too easy:
the model had paraphrased the

67
00:03:55,805 --> 00:03:59,031
ticket titles, and everything scored near
perfect.

68
00:03:59,381 --> 00:04:00,856
Useless.

69
00:04:01,206 --> 00:04:05,985
Then I had a result that said ninety-five
percent, and it was computed on

70
00:04:05,985 --> 00:04:07,556
only nineteen questions.

71
00:04:07,906 --> 00:04:10,506
One question is worth five points.

72
00:04:10,856 --> 00:04:15,750
To be confident one system really beats
another, and isn't just lucky, you'd

73
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:19,356
want something like six wins and no losses,
not one win.

74
00:04:19,706 --> 00:04:24,361
And if you see nineteen out of nineteen, the
real failure rate could still

75
00:04:24,361 --> 00:04:25,556
be sixteen percent.

76
00:04:25,906 --> 00:04:29,306
I wrote a guide on reading these numbers,
and I'll link it.

77
00:04:29,656 --> 00:04:34,415
One more thing I have to be upfront about: I
tuned on the same questions I

78
00:04:34,415 --> 00:04:37,181
scored on, so these numbers are optimistic.

79
00:04:37,801 --> 00:04:41,926
The scariest failure in RAG is the confident
wrong answer.

80
00:04:42,276 --> 00:04:45,076
So I added a floor on the reranker score.

81
00:04:45,426 --> 00:04:48,576
Below it, the system doesn't even call the
model.

82
00:04:48,926 --> 00:04:53,604
It says there's no match and suggests
opening a ticket, and that path takes

83
00:04:53,604 --> 00:04:54,851
under half a second.

84
00:04:55,201 --> 00:04:59,576
In my tests it refused all seven questions
it couldn't answer.

85
00:04:59,926 --> 00:05:05,003
The cost: it also wrongly refused three of
nineteen valid questions, about

86
00:05:05,003 --> 00:05:06,101
sixteen percent.

87
00:05:06,451 --> 00:05:09,676
That's a trade-off you choose, not a bug you
fix.

88
00:05:10,026 --> 00:05:14,918
And I broke it once: I told the model to
reply "no match" when unsure, and

89
00:05:14,918 --> 00:05:17,826
it refused eight of nineteen good questions.

90
00:05:18,176 --> 00:05:21,526
Small models take instructions very
literally.

91
00:05:22,170 --> 00:05:24,620
Tracing shows where the time goes.

92
00:05:24,970 --> 00:05:29,581
In a warm run it takes a few seconds, and
the generator is roughly three

93
00:05:29,581 --> 00:05:30,670
quarters of that.

94
00:05:31,020 --> 00:05:35,812
The query rewrite is about a fifth, and
retrieval plus reranking barely

95
00:05:35,812 --> 00:05:36,420
register.

96
00:05:36,770 --> 00:05:41,745
The first run after a cold start is slower,
because the models have to load.

97
00:05:42,095 --> 00:05:45,970
So if you want this faster, shrink the
context or the model.

98
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:48,620
Don't optimize the retrieval.

99
00:05:48,970 --> 00:05:53,845
Caveat: that's three traces, so treat the
percentages as illustrative.

100
00:05:54,464 --> 00:05:59,839
Last piece: ollmcp lets a local model decide
when to search.

101
00:06:00,189 --> 00:06:02,889
I exposed my pipeline as one tool.

102
00:06:03,239 --> 00:06:08,346
In my tests it used the tool for all
nineteen IT questions and none of the

103
00:06:08,346 --> 00:06:09,864
six chit-chat prompts.

104
00:06:10,214 --> 00:06:14,758
But the first test found only six of
nineteen correct tickets, versus

105
00:06:14,758 --> 00:06:16,339
sixteen in the pipeline.

106
00:06:16,689 --> 00:06:21,413
The reason: the model was filling in an
optional priority filter on every

107
00:06:21,413 --> 00:06:25,814
call, with a value nobody asked for, and
that hid the right tickets.

108
00:06:26,164 --> 00:06:29,539
I deleted the parameter and the results
matched.

109
00:06:29,889 --> 00:06:33,714
If you build tools for small models, keep
the schema minimal.

110
00:06:34,331 --> 00:06:35,981
Five things.

111
00:06:36,331 --> 00:06:39,456
One: read your data before you pick
techniques.

112
00:06:39,806 --> 00:06:44,481
Two: measure every stage, because the pool
limits everything after it.

113
00:06:44,831 --> 00:06:48,606
Three: test components in the full system,
not alone.

114
00:06:48,956 --> 00:06:53,906
Four: report your sample size and be honest
about tuning on your test set.

115
00:06:54,256 --> 00:06:59,631
And five: decide up front which error costs
more, wrong answers or refusals.

116
00:06:59,981 --> 00:07:04,081
The code, commands, and the number-reading
guide are linked below.

117
00:07:04,431 --> 00:07:09,119
If you want a follow-up where I test this on
questions I wrote blind, say so

118
00:07:09,119 --> 00:07:10,106
in the comments.
